sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/thompson1917-ch08-15> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Because quadrangular cells subdivide by periclinal walls that strengthen the boundary while triangular cells divide by oblique anticlines, outward growth is greater over triangular cells, so buds and outgrowths tend to arise from triangular rather than quadrangular cells, as is widely seen in cryptogams.";
    dc:type "prediction";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Outgrowths tend to arise from triangular rather than quadrangular cells";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "D’Arcy Wentworth Thompson, On Growth and Form (1st ed., Cambridge: Cambridge University Press, 1917), Chapter VIII, “The Forms of Tissues, or Cell-Aggregates (continued)”; topic “Buds from triangular cells”. Scope: growing points and buds of cryptogams (mosses, liverworts, ferns, algae)"
}
